# Tessera stdlib: the crashables of arithmetic, collections, and iterators
#
# The routines that fail on a value the caller can test for (an addition that overflows, an index past the end, an
# iterator past its last item) are failable and throw one of these. A bare call that fails stops the program on its
# line, as a crash would, and `try` in front of the call gives `None` instead: `try a + b`, `try list.getitem(i)`.
# Each holds a few numbers at most, so a `Check` or a `Maybe` of them stays one small value.

module Standard/System

### An integer result that doesn't fit its type: a sum, a difference, a product, a power, a negation.
crashable IntegerOverflowError

### A division or a remainder by zero.
crashable DivisionByZeroError

### An integer power with a negative exponent, which is a fraction.
crashable NegativeExponentError

### An index outside the elements a collection or a view holds.
crashable IndexOutOfBoundsError
    ### The index asked for.
    index: U64
    ### How many elements there are.
    count: U64

### A value asked of something that has none: an iterator past its last item, the last element of an empty list.
crashable AbsentValueError

### A key a dict doesn't hold.
crashable KeyNotFoundError

### Writes `Integer overflow: value does not fit in the target type.`
### :typeparam TWriter: The type of the Writer.
### :param out: The Writer the message goes to.
routine IntegerOverflowError.message_into[TWriter](out: @TWriter) -> None
needs TWriter: typename, Writer[TWriter]
    out.write("Integer overflow: value does not fit in the target type.")
    return

### Writes `You tried to divide by zero, which is not allowed.`
### :typeparam TWriter: The type of the Writer.
### :param out: The Writer the message goes to.
routine DivisionByZeroError.message_into[TWriter](out: @TWriter) -> None
needs TWriter: typename, Writer[TWriter]
    out.write("You tried to divide by zero, which is not allowed.")
    return

### Writes `A negative exponent makes the power a fraction, which an integer can't hold.`
### :typeparam TWriter: The type of the Writer.
### :param out: The Writer the message goes to.
routine NegativeExponentError.message_into[TWriter](out: @TWriter) -> None
needs TWriter: typename, Writer[TWriter]
    out.write("A negative exponent makes the power a fraction, which an integer can't hold.")
    return

### Writes which index was asked for and how many elements there are.
### :typeparam TWriter: The type of the Writer.
### :param out: The Writer the message goes to.
routine IndexOutOfBoundsError.message_into[TWriter](out: @TWriter) -> None
needs TWriter: typename, Writer[TWriter]
    if me.count == 0
        out.write("Index {me.index} is out of bounds: the collection is empty.")
        return
    out.write("Index {me.index} is out of bounds for {me.count} elements.")
    return

### Writes `Attempted to access a value that is absent.`
### :typeparam TWriter: The type of the Writer.
### :param out: The Writer the message goes to.
routine AbsentValueError.message_into[TWriter](out: @TWriter) -> None
needs TWriter: typename, Writer[TWriter]
    out.write("Attempted to access a value that is absent.")
    return

### Writes `Key not found.`
### :typeparam TWriter: The type of the Writer.
### :param out: The Writer the message goes to.
routine KeyNotFoundError.message_into[TWriter](out: @TWriter) -> None
needs TWriter: typename, Writer[TWriter]
    out.write("Key not found.")
    return
